FULLTEXT[5].pages.push(...[{"page": 1, "zh": "Pharmacoeconomics and Safe Medication Use\nAdrian Wong, Pharm.D., MPH, FCCP, FCCM, BCCCP\nBeth Israel Deaconess Medical Center\nBoston, Massachusetts", "en": "Pharmacoeconomics+and+ Safe+Medication+Use Adrian+Wong﹐+Pharm﹒D﹒﹐+MPH﹐+FCCP﹐+FCCM﹐+BCCCP Beth+Israel+Deaconess+Medical+Center Boston﹐+Massachusetts"}, {"page": 2, "zh": "〔原頁空白〕", "en": ""}, {"page": 3, "zh": "Pharmacoeconomics and Safe Medication Use\nAdrian Wong, Pharm.D., MPH, FCCP, FCCM, BCCCP\nBeth Israel Deaconess Medical Center\nBoston, Massachusetts", "en": "Pharmacoeconomics+and+ Safe+Medication+Use Adrian+Wong﹐+Pharm﹒D﹒﹐+MPH﹐+FCCP﹐+FCCM﹐+BCCCP Beth+Israel+Deaconess+Medical+Center Boston﹐+Massachusetts"}, {"page": 4, "zh": "## 學習目標\n1. 將 pharmacoeconomics 原則應用於 patient care。\n2. 區別 medication error、ADE、preventable ADE、ADR。\n3. 設計 ADE reporting program，包括 committee structure、committee reporting mechanisms，及 ADE detection、reporting、management 方法。\n4. 使用 2017 SCCM Safe Medication Use Guidelines for the ICU，提出改善 medication use safety 的建議。\n5. 提供 drug interaction detection 與 prevention 的 safety measures。\n6. 制訂 drug formulary proposal。\n\n## 本章縮寫\n|縮寫|英文全名|\n|---|---|\n|ADE|Adverse drug event|\n|ADR|Adverse drug reaction|\n|CDS|Clinical decision support|\n|EHR|Electronic health record|\n|P&T|Pharmacy and therapeutics (committee)|\n|QALY|Quality-adjusted life-year|\n\n## Self-Assessment Questions\n答案與解析見本章末。\n\n第 1、2 題依據以下案例。\n機構希望評估將 coagulation factor Xa（recombinant）、inactivated-zhzo 納入 formulary，以 reversal direct oral anticoagulants。\n1. 何者最適合描述此藥品的 cost 類型？\n   A. Fixed medical。\n   B. Variable medical。\n   C. Indirect。\n   D. Incremental。\n2. 機構希望使用 pharmacoeconomic data，與 prothrombin complex concentrate 比較此藥品對 neurologic disability 與 cost 的影響。文獻中應尋找哪種 analysis 最好？\n   A. Cost-benefit。\n   B. Cost-effectiveness。\n   C. Cost-minimization。\n   D. Cost-utility。\n3. 一名 60 歲病人入住 surgical ICU，病歷紀錄有 cefuroxime allergy（shortness of breath）。給予 ceftriaxone 後發生危及生命的 anaphylactic reaction。何者最適合描述此反應？\n   A. ADR。\n   B. Side effect。\n   C. ADE。\n   D. Preventable ADE。\n4. 一名 50 歲病人由 medical floor 轉入 neurology ICU，處置 apixaban-induced cerebral hemorrhage。哪個藥品最適合作為 effective tracer medication，以偵測此 ADE？\n   A. Coagulation factor VIIa。\n   B. Phytonadione。\n   C. Protamine。\n   D. Prothrombin complex concentrate。\n\n第 5、6 題依據以下案例。\n你是機構 medical ICU 新進 pharmacist，受託改善 ICU medication use safety。你已閱讀 2017 SCCM Guidelines for Safe Medication Use in the ICU，準備向 interdisciplinary critical care committee 提出建議。\n5. 依 guideline recommendations 的 grading system，考慮 strength of recommendation，改善 medication use safety 的最佳初始建議為何？\n   A. 完全整合機構 EHR systems，以改善 communication。", "en": "Learning Objectives\n\n1. Apply the principles of pharmacoeconomics to patient care.\n\n2. Differentiate between a medication error, an adverse drug event (ADE), a preventable ADE, and an adverse drug reaction (ADR).\n\n3. Design an ADE reporting program, including committee structure, committee reporting mechanisms, and methods of detecting, reporting, and managing ADEs.\n\n4. Provide recommendations for improving medication use safety using the 2017 SCCM Safe Medication Use Guidelines for the ICU.\n\n5. Provide safety measures for drug interaction detection and prevention.\n\n6. Develop a drug formulary proposal.\n\nAbbreviations in This Chapter\n\nADE Adverse drug event ADR Adverse drug reaction CDS Clinical decision support EHR Electronic health record P&T Pharmacy and therapeutics (committee) QALY Quality-adjusted life-year\n\nSelf-Assessment Questions\n\nAnswers and explanations to these questions may be found at the end of this chapter. Questions 1 and 2 pertain to the following case. Your institution wants to evaluate the addition of coagulation factor Xa (recombinant), inactivated-zhzo to your formulary for reversal of direct oral anticoagulants.\n\n1. Which best describes the type of cost this medication would have?\n\nA. Fixed medical.\n\nB. Variable medical.\n\nC. Indirect.\n\nD. Incremental.\n\n2. Your institution wants to use pharmacoeconomic data to evaluate the impact of this medication on neurologic disability and cost compared with prothrombin complex concentrate. Which analysis would be best to find in the literature?\n\nA. Cost-benefit.\n\nB. Cost-effectiveness.\n\nC. Cost-minimization.\n\nD. Cost-utility.\n\n3. A 60-year-old patient is admitted to the surgical ICU with a documented history of cefuroxime allergy (shortness of breath). The patient is administered ceftriaxone and develops a life-threatening anaphylactic reaction. Which best describes this patient’s reaction to ceftriaxone?\n\nA. Adverse drug reaction (ADR).\n\nB. Side effect.\n\nC. Adverse drug event (ADE).\n\nD. Preventable ADE.\n\n4. A 50-year-old patient is admitted to the neurology ICU from the medical floor for the management of an apixaban-induced cerebral hemorrhage. Which medication would best have been used as an effective tracer medication to help detect this ADE?\n\nA. Coagulation factor VIIa.\n\nB. Phytonadione.\n\nC. Protamine.\n\nD. Prothrombin complex concentrate. Questions 5 and 6 pertain to the following case. As a new pharmacist in the medical ICU at your institution, you have been tasked with improving the safety of medication use in the ICU. You have read the 2017 SCCM Guidelines for Safe Medication Use in the ICU and are prepared to provide recommendations to the interdisciplinary critical care committee regarding your suggestions.\n\n5. According to the grading system of the guideline recommendations, which is the best initial recommendation to improve medication use safety, given the strength of recommendation?\n\nA. Fully integrating the institution’s electronic health record (EHR) systems to improve communication."}, {"page": 5, "zh": "B. 將 interruptive CDS 整合入 EHR。\n   C. 建立 policy，要求 IV medications 只能由 pharmacists 調配。\n   D. 投資 smart intravenous infusion pumps，降低 medication errors。\n6. 機構將你的建議整合入 routine medical care 後，一名過去病人建議納入病人及其他 caregivers，以降低 medication errors。依可用文獻，何者最支持此建議？\n   A. 2017 SCCM guidelines 對納入 routine care 提供 strong recommendation。\n   B. 醫院 ICU 以外的區域，提供支持此整合的資料。\n   C. 直接觀察 medical team 與病人 interactions 的 data analyses，顯示 ICU medical errors 減少。\n   D. ICU patient chart reviews 的 data analyses 顯示，這些 reported outcomes 未被例行納入。\n7. 一名 50 歲病人，依先前 culture data，開始 meropenem 作 pneumonia empiric coverage。之後為 agitation management 開始 valproic acid，但處置無效，導致 self-extubation 及後續 reintubation。調查此 adverse event 時，哪種方法最適合評估其發生 likelihood？\n   A. Drug interaction database。\n   B. Drug interaction probability scale。\n   C. Naranjo nomogram。\n   D. Roussel Uclaf Causality Assessment Method（RUCAM）。\n8. 醫院希望將 meropenem／vaborbactam 納入 formulary。此為針對高度 drug-resistant organisms 的 broad-spectrum antimicrobial，FDA 核准用於 complicated UTIs。最適合的 use restriction 為何？\n   A. 限 intensivist 使用。\n   B. 限 hospital-acquired pneumonia 病人。\n   C. 限經 infectious diseases clinicians 核准。\n   D. 限有適當 cardiac monitoring 的病人。", "en": "B. Integrating interruptive clinical decision support (CDS) into the EHR.\n\nC. Creating a policy requiring that intravenous medications can only be prepared by pharmacists.\n\nD. Investing in smart intravenous infusion pumps to reduce medication errors.\n\n6. After your institution has integrated your recommendation into routine medical care, a former patient recommends including the patient and other caregivers in reducing medication errors. According to the available literature, which best supports this recommendation?\n\nA. The 2017 SCCM guidelines provide a strong recommendation for this integration into routine care.\n\nB. Areas in the hospital outside the ICU provide data supporting this integration.\n\nC. Data analyses on direct observations of interactions between the medical team and patients have shown a reduction in medical errors in the ICU.\n\nD. Data analyses on chart reviews of ICU patients have shown that these reported outcomes are not routinely accounted for.\n\n7. A 50-year-old patient was initiated on meropenem for empiric coverage of pneumonia, given prior culture data. They were subsequently initiated on valproic acid for the management of agitation; however, this was not effective for management and resulted in self-extubation and subsequent reintubation. In investigating this adverse event, which method would best evaluate the likelihood of its occurrence?\n\nA. Drug interaction database.\n\nB. Drug interaction probability scale.\n\nC. Naranjo nomogram.\n\nD. Roussel Uclaf Causality Assessment Method (RUCAM).\n\n8. Your hospital wants to add meropenem/vaborbactam, a broad-spectrum antimicrobial targeting highly drug-resistant organisms that is FDA approved for complicated UTIs, to the formulary. Which would be the most appropriate restriction to its use?\n\nA. Restriction to intensivist use.\n\nB. Restriction to patients with hospital-acquired pneumonia.\n\nC. Restriction to infectious diseases clinicians’ approval.\n\nD. Restriction to patients with appropriate cardiac monitoring."}, {"page": 6, "zh": "## BPS Critical Care Pharmacy Examination Content Outline\n本章涵蓋以下部分：\n1. Domain 2：Therapeutics and Patient Management\n   a. Task A：1、5–7\n   b. Task C：2、4、5\n2. Domain 3：Professional Practice\n   a. Task A：1–6\n   b. Task B：1", "en": "BPS Critical Care Pharmacy Examination Content Outline This chapter covers the following sections of the Critical Care Pharmacy Examination Content Outline:\n\n1. Domain 2: Therapeutics and Patient Management\n\na. Task A: 1, 5–7\n\nb. Task C: 2, 4, 5\n\n2. Domain 3: Professional Practice\n\na. Task A: 1-6\n\nb. Task B: 1"}, {"page": 7, "zh": "## I. PHARMACOECONOMICS — 藥物經濟學\nA. 概述\n1. Pharmacoeconomics 是描述並分析 drugs、pharmaceutical services 的 costs，以及其對 individuals、health care systems 及／或 society 的 effects。\n2. Economic evaluations 辨識、測量並比較 pharmaceutical product 或 service 的 costs 與 consequences。依評估的 disease state／therapy、alternative therapies、perspective（例如 payer、provider、patient、society）及 intended outcome，可能傳達不同結果。\n   a. 完整 pharmacoeconomic analyses 包括：\n      i. Cost-benefit。\n      ii. Cost-effectiveness。\n      iii. Cost-minimization。\n      iv. Cost-utility。\n   b. Humanistic outcomes 或 patient-reported outcome measures 包括：\n      i. Functional status。\n      ii. Health status。\n      iii. Health-related quality of life。\n      iv. Patient satisfaction。\n      v. Quality of life。\n\nB. Costs 類型\n1. Costs versus charges。\n   a. 文獻常單獨使用 charges 一詞，但 charges 實際為 costs（亦稱 expenses）加 profit；或特定使用項目的 acquisition cost（例如給予的 medication dose 成本）加提供 service 的估計成本（例如 medication use process 相關的 workflow、personnel）再加 markup。\n2. Costs 類型（Table 1）。\n\n### Table 1. Costs 類型\n|Cost 類型|定義|範例|\n|---|---|---|\n|Direct|與預防、偵測或治療 disease／illness 相關的 costs|以下 medical 與 non-medical costs|\n|• Medical|與 medical care 相關的 costs|Fixed：保持固定、不依 use volume 變動，通常不納入 pharmacoeconomic evaluations，例如 rent、salaried workers。Variable：依 use volume 變動，例如 laboratory tests、medications、procedures。|\n|• Non-medical|因 disease／illness 產生、但不涉及購買 medical services 的 costs|Child care、specialty diets、transportation。|\n|Indirect|與 morbidity／mortality 相關，因 disease／illness 導致 productivity 變動的 costs|因無法工作產生的 costs、因 premature death 損失的 income。|\n|Intangible|Disease 的 nonfinancial outcomes 相關 costs|ICU survival 後 posttraumatic stress disorder 的 costs；post-ICU syndrome。|\n|Incremental|購買 medical care 額外 benefit／effect 的 additional costs|為控制在 ICU 新發生的 atrial fibrillation，增加額外 medications。|\n\n|Total|包含上述全部 costs|N/A。|\nN/A = not applicable。Table 1 為 economic evaluations 使用的 cost 類型摘要。", "en": "I. PHARMACOECONOMICS\n\nA. Overview\n\n1. Pharmacoeconomics is the description and analysis of the costs of drugs and pharmaceutical services as well as their effects on individuals, health care systems, and/or society.\n\n2. Economic evaluations identify, measure, and compare the costs and consequences of a pharmaceutical product or service. These can convey different results, depending on disease state/therapy being evaluated, alternative therapies, perspective (e.g., payer, provider, patient, society), and intended outcome.\n\na. Full pharmacoeconomic analyses include the following:\n\ni. Cost-benefit ii. Cost-effectiveness iii. Cost-minimization iv. Cost-utility\n\nb. Humanistic outcomes or patient-reported outcome measures include the following:\n\ni. Functional status ii. Health status iii. Health-related quality of life iv. Patient satisfaction\n\nv. Quality of life\n\nB. Types of Costs\n\n1. Costs versus charges\n\na. Although the term charges alone is often used in the literature, charges are actually costs (also termed expenses) plus profit or a combination of the acquisition cost for a specific item used (e.g., cost of medication dose administered) plus estimated costs to provide the service (e.g., workflow and personnel associated with the process for medication use) plus a markup.\n\n2. Types of cost (Table 1) Type of Cost Definition Examples Direct • Medical • Non-medical Costs associated with prevention, detection, or treatment of a disease or illness Costs associated with medical care Costs as a result of disease or illness that do not involve the purchase of medical services Fixed (costs that remain constant and are not dependent on volume of use; not typically included in pharmacoeconomic evaluations): Rent, salaried workers Variable (costs depend on volume of use): Laboratory tests, medications, procedures Child care, specialty diets, transportation Indirect Costs associated with morbidity or mortality, related to change in productivity as a result of disease or illness Costs because of inability to work, income lost because of premature death Intangible Costs associated with nonfinancial outcomes of disease Costs from posttraumatic stress disorder from ICU survival; post-ICU syndrome Incremental Additional costs to purchase additional benefit or effect of medical care Additional medications to control atrial fibrillation that started in ICU Total Inclusion of all the above costs N/A\n\nTable 1. Summary of Types of Cost Used in Economic Evaluations N/A = not applicable."}, {"page": 8, "zh": "C. Pharmacoeconomic Evaluations 的類型（Table 2）\n1. Full economic evaluation 有數種類型；主要差別在於如何測量及評價個人獲得的 benefits。\n2. Cost analysis\n   a. 僅比較兩種或更多 treatments／services 的 cost（不含 effectiveness）。\n   b. Effectiveness 未知時，可能比 cost-minimization analysis 更適用。\n3. Cost-benefit analysis\n   a. 系統性評估 treatments／programs 的 strengths、weaknesses，以確定在減少 expense 的同時達成 benefits 的最佳方式。\n   b. Costs 與 outcomes 均以 monetary units 測量，包括 direct、indirect benefits，允許直接比較。\n   c. 辨識 investment yield（例如 post-ICU clinic 的需求論證，或 pharmacokinetics service 與 vaccination program 比較）。\n   d. Results 可用 cost-benefit ratio、net cost 或 net benefit 表達。\n4. Cost-effectiveness analysis\n   a. 比較兩種或更多使用 resources（例如 treatments、services）的 treatment alternatives／programs。\n   b. Costs 以 monetary terms 測量，outcomes 則以 effectiveness units 測量。\n   c. 確定哪種 alternative 可產生 desired effect 或最大化 effect；可能是較昂貴、但提供比便宜選項更理想 outcome 的選項。\n   d. Results 以 incremental cost-effectiveness ratio（ICER）表達，定義為相對下一個最有效 intervention，多產生一單位 output 所需的 additional cost。可用 cost-effectiveness plane 呈現，將 ICER 繪於四個 quadrants（x-axis 為 effectiveness，y-axis 為 cost）。\n5. Cost-minimization analysis\n   a. 比較兩種或更多 treatment alternatives／programs，除 costs 外，outcomes（例如 effectiveness）幾乎相同；因此 results 以 monetary form 表達，方便檢視 resource outcomes。\n   b. 比較 costs，以確定具有 noninferior efficacy 的最便宜 alternative。\n   c. 若沒有 evidence 支持 treatment alternative effectiveness，不應使用。\n6. Cost-utility analysis\n   a. 比較兩種或更多 treatment alternatives／programs，costs 以 monetary terms 測量，outcome 以 patient preferences、quality of life 或 QALYs 表達。\n      i. 是一種 cost-effectiveness analysis，為 outcome 指派 values（utilities）。\n      ii. Life 與 death 為 utilities 的 reference states，perfect health = 1.0、death = 0.0；但對病人而言，某種 state（例如 vegetative）可能比死亡更糟，因此可有 negative utility。\n      iii. Utilities 或病人對 disease state 的 preference，可透過文獻資訊、convenience sample experts、直接 decision theory 方法，或間接 psychometric methods 測量。\n         (a) Direct approaches 包括 rating scales、time trade-off（acute、chronic conditions）、standard gamble（acute、chronic conditions）。\n         (b) Indirect approaches 包括 multi-attribute health state scores（例如 quality-of-life assessment tools）。\n   b. 計算 QALYs 時，先確定 life-years gained，再乘以該 life-year 的 utility。例如預期壽命 20 年、但有 disability，自認功能僅為能力 70%，則 20 年 × 0.7 = 14 QALYs。\n   c. 使用 quality-of-life assessment tools 測量病人的 function 與 overall well-being，例如 ICU 使用的一般及／或 disease-specific tools（EuroQol 5D Questionnaire、European Organization for Research and Treatment of Cancer 8 Dimensions）。", "en": "C. Types of Pharmacoeconomic Evaluations (Table 2)\n\n1. There are several types of full economic evaluation. The main difference between the types of full economic evaluation is how the benefits to the individual are measured and valued.\n\n2. Cost analysis\n\na. Compares only cost (i.e., no effectiveness) of two or more treatments or services\n\nb. Potentially more applicable than cost-minimization analysis when effectiveness is not known\n\n3. Cost-benefit analysis\n\na. Systematic approach assessing strengths and weaknesses of treatments or programs to determine the best approach to attaining benefits while minimizing expense\n\nb. Both costs and outcomes are measured in monetary units, including direct and indirect benefits, allowing for a direct comparison.\n\nc. Identifies yield of investment (e.g., justification of post-ICU clinic, comparison of a pharmacokinetics service with a vaccination program)\n\nd. Results may be expressed with cost-benefit ratio, net cost, or net benefit.\n\n4. Cost-effectiveness analysis\n\na. Compares two or more treatment alternatives or programs in which resources (e.g., treatments, services) are used\n\nb. Costs are measured in monetary terms, whereas outcomes are measured in units of effectiveness.\n\nc. Determines which alternative can produce the desired effect or maximization of effect, which may by a more expensive option that provides a more desirable outcome than a less expensive option\n\nd. Results are expressed as the incremental cost-effectiveness ratio (ICER), which is defined as the additional cost to the next most effective intervention producing another unit of output. These results can be displayed in a cost-effectiveness plane, which graphs the ICER in four quadrants (x-axis is effectiveness; y-axis is cost).\n\n5. Cost-minimization analysis\n\na. Compares two or more treatment alternatives or programs for which the outcomes are virtually identical (e.g., effectiveness) except for costs; therefore, results are expressed in monetary form, which allows for ease in examining resource outcomes\n\nb. Costs are compared to determine the least expensive alternative with a noninferior efficacy.\n\nc. Should not be used if no evidence supports the effectiveness of the treatment alternative\n\n6. Cost-utility analysis\n\na. Comparison of two or more treatment alternatives or programs in which costs are measured in monetary terms and outcome is expressed with respect to patient preferences, quality of life, or quality-adjusted life-years (QALYs)\n\ni. Form of cost-effectiveness analysis in which values (utilities) are assigned to the outcome ii. Life and death are reference states for the utilities, with perfect health = 1.0 and death = 0.0 (although to a patient, a certain state [e.g., vegetative] may be worse than death, allowing for a negative utility). iii. Utilities or a patient’s preference for a disease state can be measured by obtaining information from the literature, approaching a convenience sample of experts, using decision theory with a direct approach, or using psychometric methods with indirect approaches.\n\n(a) Direct approaches include rating scales, time trade-off (acute and chronic conditions), and standard gamble (acute and chronic conditions).\n\n(b) Indirect approaches include multi-attribute health state scores (e.g., quality-of-life assessment tools).\n\nb. To calculate QALYs, the life-years gained is determined and then multiplied by the utility of that life-year. For example, if persons have a life expectancy of 20 years but have a disability and believe they are functioning at only 70% of their capacity, the QALY is 20 years × 0.7 = 14 QALYs.\n\nc. Measures the function and overall well-being of patients using quality-of-life assessment tools, such as those used in the ICU, which are general and/or disease-specific (e.g., EuroQol 5D Questionnaire, European Organization for Research and Treatment of Cancer 8 Dimensions)"}, {"page": 9, "zh": "### Table 2. Pharmacoeconomic Evaluations 摘要ᵃ\n|Economic evaluation|Cost measurement|Consequences／outcomes|Consequences／outcomes 的 measurement／valuation|範例|Challenges|\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n|Cost analysis（partial evaluation）|Monetary|不考慮，故非 full economic evaluation。|不考慮，故非 full economic evaluation。|ARDS 病人 cisatracurium 與 rocuronium 的 cost 比較，不考慮任何 outcome。|與 CMA、CBA、CEA、CUA 相比 challenges 有限。|\n|Cost-benefit analysis（CBA）|Total cost of care，以 monetary 表達。|Single 或 multiple effect／outcome，不必是兩 alternatives 共有；effects 轉為 monetary units，使不同 alternatives 可比較。|Monetary units。|Pharmacist-led blood factor prescription 與 standard of care 對醫院 andexanet costs 的比較。|將 consequences 轉為 monetary units。|\n|Cost-effectiveness analysis（CEA）|Total cost of care，以 monetary 表達。|所有 alternatives 必須有單一共同關注的 effect／outcome，但達成程度不同；alternative strategies outcomes 不等同，以單向 natural units 測量。|Nonmonetary physical effectiveness units（例如 life-years gained、surrogate endpoint）。|Andexanet 與 4F-PCC 對 bleeding-related mortality 的比較。|確定 decision tree clinical pathway 的 probabilities，以確定 consequences。|\n|Cost-minimization analysis（CMA）|Total cost of care，以 monetary 表達。|必須評估；所有 alternatives 共同關注的單一 effect／outcome，已證明 equivalent。|Identical。|因假定 equivalent outcomes，比較藥品與其 biosimilar 的 cost。|證明 consequences identical。|\n|Cost-utility analysis（CUA）|Total cost of care，以 monetary 表達。|Single 或 multiple effect／outcome，不必兩 alternatives 共有；effects 轉為 QALYs，使不同 alternatives 可比較。|Utility values 與 QALYs。|比較 andexanet 與 4F-PCC 的 patient outcomes，以 patient utility（QALY）測量。|測量 utilities。|\nᵃ Andexanet = coagulation factor Xa（recombinant）、inactivated-zhzo。\n4F-PCC = 4-factor prothrombin complex concentrate；QALY = quality-adjusted life-year。\n修改自：Drummond MF, Sculpher MJ, Claxton K, et al. Methods for the Economic Evaluation of Health Care Programmes, 4th ed. Oxford University Press, 2015.", "en": "a A n d e x a n et = c o a g ul ati o n f a ct or X a (r e c o m bi n a nt), i n a cti v at e d- z h z o. 4 F- P C C = 4-f a ct or pr ot hr o m bi n c o m pl e x c o n c e ntr at e; Q A L Y = q u alit y- a dj ust e d lif e- y e ar. M o di fi e d fr o m: Dr u m m o n d M F, S c ul p h er MJ, Cl a xt o n K, et al. M et h o ds f or t h e E c o n o mi c E v al u ati o n of H e alt h C ar e Pr o gr a m m es, 4t h e d. O xf or d U ni v ersit y Pr ess, 2 0 1 5. T a bl e 2. S u m m a r y o f P h a r m a c o e c o n o m i c E v al u ati o n s a E c o n o mi c E v al u a ti o n C o s t M e a s u r e m e n t C o n s e q u e n c e s o r O u t c o m e s M e a s u r e m e n t / V al u a ti o n o f C o n s e q u e n c e o r O u t c o m e s E x a m pl e C h all e n g e s C o st a n al y si s ( p a r ti al e v al u ati o n) M o n et a r y N ot c o n si d e r e d, s o n ot a f ull e c o n o m i c e v al u ati o n N ot c o n si d e r e d, s o n ot a f ull e c o n o m i c e v al u ati o n C o st o f ci s at r a c u r i u m c o m p a r e d wit h r o c u r o ni u m i n p ati e nt s wit h a c ut e r e s pi r at o r y di st r e s s s y n d r o m e ( di s r e g a r di n g a n y o ut c o m e) C h all e n g e s li m it e d c o m p a r e d wit h C M A, C B A, C E A, C U A C o st - b e n e fit a n al y si s ( C B A) M o n et a r y f o r t ot al c o st o f c a r e Si n gl e o r m ulti pl e e f f e ct / o ut c o m e t h at d o e s n ot h a v e t o b e c o m m o n t o b ot h alt e r n ati v e s E f f e ct s a r e t r a n sl at e d t o m o n et a r y u nit s, s o c o m p a r i s o n a m o n g v a r i o u s alt e r n ati v e s i s p o s si bl e M o n et a r y u nit s C o m p a r i s o n o f p h a r m a ci st -l e d bl o o d f a ct o r p r e s c r i pti o n wit h st a n d a r d o f c a r e o n a n d e x a n et c o st s t o h o s pit al Tr a n sl ati n g c o n s e q u e n c e s t o m o n et a r y u nit s C o st - e f f e cti v e n e s s a n al y si s ( C E A) M o n et a r y f o r t ot al c o st o f c a r e M u st h a v e a si n gl e e f f e ct / o ut c o m e o f i nt e r e st f o r all alt e r n ati v e s c o n si d e r e d, a n d t h e e f f e ct / o ut c o m e i s a c hi e v e d t o a di f f e r e nt d e g r e e T h e o ut c o m e s o f t h e alt e r n ati v e st r at e gi e s a r e n ot e q ui v al e nt a n d a r e m e a s u r e d i n u ni di r e cti o n al n at u r al u nit s N o n m o n et a r y p h y si c al u nit s o f e f f e cti v e n e s s. ( e. g., li f e - y e a r s g ai n e d, s u r r o g at e e n d p oi nt) C o m p a r i s o n o f a n d e x a n et wit h 4 F - P C C o n bl e e di n g - r el at e d m o r t alit y D et e r m i ni n g t h e p r o b a biliti e s o f t h e cli ni c al p at h w a y f o r t h e d e ci si o n t r e e t o d et e r m i n e t h e c o n s e q u e n c e s C o st - m i ni m i z ati o n a n al y si s ( C M A) M o n et a r y f o r t ot al c o st o f c a r e M u st b e e v al u at e d Si n gl e e f f e ct / o ut c o m e o f i nt e r e st f o r all alt e r n ati v e s t h at w e r e s h o w n t o b e e q ui v al e nt I d e nti c al C o m p a r i s o n o f m e di c ati o n a n d it s bi o si m il a r o n c o st b e c a u s e e q ui v al e nt o ut c o m e s a r e a s s u m e d P r o vi n g c o n s e q u e n c e s a r e i d e nti c al C o st - utilit y a n al y si s ( C U A) M o n et a r y f o r t ot al c o st o f c a r e Si n gl e o r m ulti pl e e f f e ct / o ut c o m e t h at d o e s n ot h a v e t o b e c o m m o n t o b ot h alt e r n ati v e s E f f e ct s a r e t r a n sl at e d t o Q A L Y s, s o c o m p a r i s o n a m o n g v a r i o u s alt e r n ati v e s i s p o s si bl e Utilit y v al u e s a n d Q A L Y s C o m p a r i s o n o f a n d e x a n et wit h 4 F - P C C o n p ati e nt o ut c o m e s, m e a s u r e d b y p ati e nt utilit y ( Q A L Y ) M e a s u r i n g utiliti e s"}, {"page": 10, "zh": "D. Pharmacoeconomics 的應用\n1. Formulary management：確定新上市或核准 medications 應納入／排除，以及有／無可能影響 prescribing patterns 的 use criteria。\n2. 制訂 clinical guidelines、policies、protocols，在 safety 與 efficacy 情境下促進最 cost-effective、理想的 medication use。\n3. 實施 drug use policy，促進 health care products、services 的最有效率使用。\n4. 執行 program evaluations，確定現有 medical／pharmacy service 的 value，或開始新 service 的 potential worth；亦指導建立關鍵 business objectives，或 growth、adoption、process control metrics。\n5. Population health analytics（例如使用 pharmacoeconomic analyses，確定 health system 內 EBM treatment decisions）。\n6. 確定個別病人 treatment decisions。\n7. 發表有 pharmacist involvement 的 pharmacoeconomic evaluations。\n\n## Patient Case\n1. 一名 30 歲病人因 motor vehicle accident 入住 surgical ICU。從病人 perspective，何者最能示範 indirect cost？\n   A. 作為 engineer 的 wages 損失。\n   B. Accident 引發 posttraumatic stress disorder 的 effects。\n   C. 為 pain management 給予的 fentanyl。\n   D. 運送其至醫院的 ambulance cost。\n\n## II. DRUG-RELATED EVENTS：MEDICATION ERRORS、ADEs、ADRs、PREVENTABLE ADEs\nA. 概述\n1. 在美國，每年 770,000 起 injuries／deaths 由 ADRs 引起；每家醫院 ADEs cost burden 超過 $5.6 million。\n   a. 至多 60% hospital readmissions 可歸因 medication access issues 或 ADEs — 此資料在 EHR data explosion 之前，若 EHR 使用不協調，可能更糟。\n   b. Severe ADE 增加 hospital LOS 8–12 天，每位病人成本 $16,000–$24,000。\n   c. 美國 preventable ADEs 的 cost burden 為每家醫院每年 $4.1 million。Medical malpractice lawsuits 經常與 ADEs 相關或由其引起。\n2. 病人 critically ill 時，接受 high-risk medication 的機會較高，且因 altered pharmacokinetics、organ failure 對藥品反應改變，ADEs risk 增加。\n3. TJC medication management standard 要求醫院回應 actual／potential ADEs、significant ADRs、medication errors。至少需 response、document、report、manage ADEs。Pharmacist 作為 drug expert，應在 prospective order verification 扮演重要角色，必要時 intervention 以預防 ADEs。", "en": "D. Application of Pharmacoeconomics\n\n1. Formulary management to determine whether newly marketed or approved medications should be included/excluded with/without use criteria that may affect prescribing patterns\n\n2. Development of clinical guidelines, policies, or protocols to promote the most cost-effective and desirable use of medications in the context of safety and efficacy\n\n3. Implementation of drug use policy to promote the most efficient use of health care products and services\n\n4. Perform program evaluations to determine the value of an existing medical or pharmacy service or the potential worth of starting a new service, in addition to guiding the establishment of key business objectives or metrics of growth, adoption, or process control\n\n5. Population health analytics (e.g., use of pharmacoeconomic analyses to determine evidence-based medicine treatment decisions within a health system)\n\n6. Determine individual patient treatment decisions\n\n7. Publication of pharmacoeconomic evaluations with pharmacist involvement\n\nPatient Case\n\n1. A 30-year-old patient is admitted to the surgical ICU after a motor vehicle accident. Which would best exemplify an indirect cost from their perspective?\n\nA. Loss of wages from occupation as an engineer.\n\nB. Effects of posttraumatic stress disorder from the accident.\n\nC. Fentanyl administered to manage pain.\n\nD. Cost of the ambulance that transported them to the hospital.\n\nII. DRUG-RELATED EVENTS: MEDICATION ERRORS, ADES AND ADRS, PREVENTABLE ADES\n\nA. Overview\n\n1. In the United States, 770,000 injuries or deaths are caused by ADRs annually; the cost burden of ADEs is greater than $5.6 million per hospital.\n\na. Up to 60% of hospital readmissions are attributable to either medication access issues or ADEs – This was before the data explosion with the EHR, which may have made it worse if it is used in discordant fashion.\n\nb. A severe ADE increases the hospital length of stay by 8–12 days at a cost of $16,000–$24,000 per patient.\n\nc. The cost burden of preventable ADEs in the United States is $4.1 million per hospital annually. Medical malpractice lawsuits are often related to, or occur because of, ADEs.\n\n2. The risk of ADEs increases when patients are critically ill, have a higher chance of being administered a high-risk medication, and have altered response to medications because of altered pharmacokinetics and organ failure.\n\n3. The Joint Commission medication management standard requires hospitals to respond to actual or potential ADEs, significant ADRs, and medication errors. At the very least, hospitals need to respond, document and report, and manage ADEs. The pharmacist, who is the drug expert, should play an integral role in prospective order verification to prevent ADEs by intervening as needed."}, {"page": 11, "zh": "B. Drug-Related Events 的定義\n1. Figure 1 摘要 drug-related events。\n\n### Figure 1. Drug-related events 類型\n圖詳細呈現 medication errors、ADEs、ADRs。最大 circle 為所有給予的 medications；次大 circle 為給藥相關、且已到達病人的 medication errors。第三大 circle 為 ADEs，可能由 medication errors 造成（circles 重疊）。Medication errors 造成的 ADEs 因而為 preventable。最小 circle 為 ADRs，是造成病人 harm／injury 的一種 ADE；其中部分為 medication errors 的結果，如 Medication Errors 與 ADR circles 的重疊。\n\n2. Medication errors\n   a. Medication use process 的 mishaps（例如 reconciling、prescribing／ordering、transcribing、dispensing、administering、monitoring）。\n      i. 發生前攔截並阻止的 medication errors，稱「near misses」。\n      ii. 有些 medication errors 造成 harm（產生 ADE），有些不會。Medication errors 應透過 health system patient incident reporting system 報告。Harm 為 physical／psychologic injury，或對 patient health 的 damage，包括 temporary 與 permanent injury。\n   b. Medication errors 可分類為：\n      i. Category A：無 error — 可能導致 error 的 scenarios。\n      ii. Category B：發生 error，但未到達病人。\n      iii. Category C：發生 error 並到達病人，但未造成 harm。\n      iv. Category D：發生 error 並到達病人，需要 monitoring 確認未造成 harm，或需要 intervention 預防 harm。\n      v. Category E：發生 error，可能促成或導致 temporary harm，且需要 intervention。\n      vi. Category F：發生 error，可能促成或導致 temporary harm，且需要初次或延長 hospitalization。\n      vii. Category G：發生 error，可能促成或導致 permanent patient harm。", "en": "B. Definitions of Drug-Related Events\n\n1. Figure 1 summarizes drug-related events.\n\nFigure 1. Types of drug-related events. This figure details types of drug-related events including medication errors, adverse drug events (ADEs), and adverse drug reactions (ADRs). The largest circle illustrates all medications administered, with the next largest circle indicating which medications administered were associated with a medication error that reached the patient. The third largest circle illustrates ADEs, which may be a result of medication errors (i.e., overlap between circles). ADEs that are a result of medication errors are therefore preventable. The smallest circle illustrates ADRs, which are a type of ADE that causes patient harm/injury. Some of these are the result of medication errors, as suggested by the overlap between the “Medication Errors” and “ADR” circles.\n\n2. Medication errors\n\na. Mishaps that occur during the medication use process (e.g., reconciling, prescribing/ordering, transcribing, dispensing, administering, monitoring)\n\ni. Medication errors that are intercepted and stopped before they occur are called “near misses.” ii. Some medication errors cause harm (result in an ADE), and some do not. Medication errors should be reported through the health system’s patient incident reporting system. Harm is physical or psychologic injury or damage to a patient’s health, including both temporary and permanent injury.\n\nb. Medications errors can be categorized as the following:\n\ni. Category A: No error – Scenarios that can cause error ii. Category B: An error occurred but did not reach the patient iii. Category C: An error occurred and reached the patient but did not cause harm iv. Category D: An error occurred that reached the patient and required monitoring to confirm that it resulted in no harm to the patient or required intervention to prevent harm\n\nv. Category E: An error occurred that may have contributed to or resulted in temporary harm to the patient and required intervention vi. Category F: An error occurred that may have contributed to or resulted in temporary harm to the patient and required initial or prolonged hospitalization vii. Category G: An error occurred that may have contributed to or resulted in permanent patient harm"}, {"page": 12, "zh": "viii. Category H：發生 error，需要維持生命所必需的 intervention。\n      ix. Category I：發生 error，可能促成或導致病人死亡。\n   c. TJC 將造成 severe temporary harm（需 intervention）、permanent harm 或 death 的 medication errors 定義為 sentinel event（例如可能 Category E，以及 Categories F–I）。\n   d. 可透過 ISMP Medication Error Reporting Program 報告。\n      i. Confidential、自願性 system，由 ISMP staff 調查，再報告至 FDA MedWatch program 與 product vendors。\n      ii. Vaccines 使用個別 reporting method，即 ISMP Vaccine Errors Reporting Program；vaccine medication error reports 可選擇提交 manufacturer name、dosage、lot number、expiration date、National Drug Code。\n      iii. Consumers 也可透過 ISMP 報告 medication errors。\n      iv. Medication errors 可經 MedWatch system 直接報告至 FDA。FDA MedWatch 允許 ADE 與 medication error reporting。\n3. Adverse drug events\n   a. 因 drug use 造成的 injuries，包括 drug 本身造成的 harm（ADRs、overdoses），以及 drug 使用造成的 harm，包括 dose changes 與停用 drug therapy。\n   b. 整體而言，ADE 是較廣的詞，描述任何與 medication 相關的 harmful event，包括 overdose 等不適當使用；ADR 則用於 medication 正常使用時發生的 adverse response，包括 harm。\n4. Preventable ADEs\n   a. 發現 standard professional behavior、practice、technique 遭違反，未採必要 precautions，或透過修改 behavior、practice、technique、care 可預防的事件，屬 preventable ADE。\n      i. 由任何到達病人並造成 harm 的 medication error 引起。\n      ii. 所有 ADEs 約 30%–50% 可預防。\n      iii. Drug interactions 占所有 preventable in-hospital ADRs 的 3%–5%（section IV 進一步討論）。\n   b. Preventable ADEs 範例：\n      i. 對 Asian bipolar disorder 病人開立 carbamazepine，未檢測 HLA-B*1502 allele，之後發生 carbamazepine-induced toxic epidermal necrolysis。\n      ii. 給予 heparin 未採 weight-based dosing，造成 supratherapeutic partial thromboplastin time 與 intracranial hemorrhage。\n      iii. Phenytoin 不慎與 dextrose 而非 normal saline 混合，造成 precipitation、缺乏 drug potency，導致 withdrawal seizures 與 status epilepticus。\n5. Nonpreventable ADEs\n   a. 與 medication error 無關、無已知 mitigation strategies 的 unintended reactions，導致 patient harm（例如適當 anticoagulant dosing、administration、monitoring 下仍出血）。\n6. Adverse drug reactions\n   a. 定義\n      i. WHO 定義 ADRs 為「任何有害且非預期的 drug response，發生於通常用於 disease prophylaxis、diagnosis、therapy，或 modification of physiological function 的 doses」。", "en": "viii. Category H: An error occurred that required intervention necessary to sustain life ix. Category I: An error occurred that may have contributed to or resulted in the patient’s death\n\nc. Medication errors are defined by The Joint Commission as a sentinel event that includes events such as those that cause severe temporary harm (requiring intervention), permanent harm, or death (e.g., potentially category E, categories F–I).\n\nd. May be reported through the Institute for Safe Medication Practices (ISMP Medication Error Reporting Program)\n\ni. A confidential and voluntary system, investigated by ISMP staff and then reported to the FDA’s MedWatch program and product vendors ii. A separate reporting method is used for vaccines, specifically the ISMP Vaccine Errors Reporting Program. Vaccine medication error reports provide an option to submit the vaccine’s manufacturer name, dosage, lot number, expiration date, and National Drug Code. iii. Consumers may also report medication errors through ISMP. iv. Medication errors may be reported directly to the FDA by the MedWatch system. The FDA’s MedWatch allows ADE and medication error reporting.\n\n3. Adverse drug events\n\na. Injuries resulting from use of a drug, which include harm caused by the drug (ADRs and overdoses) and harm from use of the drug, including dose changes and discontinuations of drug therapy\n\nb. Overall, ADE is the broader term used to describe any harmful event associated with a medication, including inappropriate use such as an overdose, whereas ADR is used when an adverse response, including harm, occurs with normal use of the medication.\n\n4. Preventable ADEs\n\na. A preventable ADE occurs when a breach of standard professional behavior, practice, or technique was identified or when necessary precautions were not taken, or when the event was preventable by modifying behavior, practice, technique, or care.\n\ni. Results from any medication error that reaches the patient and causes harm ii. About 30%–50% of all ADEs are preventable. iii. Drug interactions account for 3%–5% of all preventable in-hospital ADRs (drug interactions are discussed further in section IV, Drug Interaction Surveillance and Prevention).\n\nb. Examples of preventable ADEs\n\ni. Carbamazepine prescribed for an Asian patient with bipolar disorder without testing for the HLA-B*1502 allele; in turn, the patient develops carbamazepine-induced toxic epidermal necrolysis ii. Heparin administered without the use of weight-based dosing, causing a supratherapeutic partial thromboplastin time and an intracranial hemorrhage iii. Phenytoin mixed accidentally with dextrose rather than normal saline, causing precipitation and lack of drug potency and leading to a patient developing withdrawal seizures and status epilepticus\n\n5. Nonpreventable ADEs\n\na. ADEs not associated with a medication error and are unintended reactions without known mitigation strategies resulting in patient harm (a patient with a bleed despite appropriate dosing, administration, and monitoring of the anticoagulant)\n\n6. Adverse drug reactions\n\na. Definitions\n\ni. WHO defines ADRs as “any response to a drug which is noxious and unintended, and which occurs at doses normally used for prophylaxis, diagnosis, or therapy of disease, or for the modification of physiological function.”"}, {"page": 13, "zh": "ii. Karch 與 Lasagna（JAMA 1975;234:1236-41）定義 ADRs 為「任何有害且非預期的 drug response，發生於人類用於 prophylaxis、diagnosis、therapy 的 doses，但排除未達預期目的的 therapeutic failure」。\n      iii. 兩定義主要差異：WHO 定義將 therapeutic failures 視為 unintended effect 與 ADR；Karch、Lasagna 定義則不包含。兩者均指通常人類使用的 doses，因此排除 overdoses 與超過 normal doses 的 medication errors。\n      iv. Therapeutic failures 所致 ADRs，若有易取得且詳細的資料紀錄 drug、therapeutic failure，可顯著影響 patient care。\n   b. 依定義的範例\n      i. WHO\n         (a) Clopidogrel 未能預防 ischemic stroke。\n         (b) Enoxaparin 未能預防 deep vein thrombosis。\n         (c) Famotidine 未能預防 IV ketorolac-induced gastropathy。\n         (d) Pantoprazole 未能預防重症病人 stress ulcer 引起的 GI bleeding。\n      ii. Karch、Lasagna\n         (a) 用 lorazepam 治療 anxiety，可能造成 sedation。\n            (1) 此為 unintended adverse reaction；反之，在重症病人用 lorazepam 達到 sedation 為 intended effect，不是 ADR。\n         (b) 用 diphenhydramine 治療 allergic rhinitis 時，造成 unintended sedation。\n            (1) 此為 ADR；反之，將 diphenhydramine 作 sedative hypnotic 為 intended effect，不是 ADR。\n\nC. FDA-Reportable ADEs\n1. 向 FDA 報告 ADE 時，FDA 將 ADEs 定義為 drugs／devices 相關的 serious adverse events，且「patient outcome 為 death、life-threatening（真正有死亡風險）、hospitalization（初次或延長）、disability（重大、持續、永久）、congenital anomaly，或需要 intervention 預防 permanent impairment／damage」。FDA-reportable serious ADEs 可包括：\n   a. Acetaminophen-induced hepatotoxicity。\n   b. Apixaban-induced intracranial hemorrhage。\n   c. Gentamicin-induced irreversible ototoxicity。\n2. FDA 也關注新上市 drugs 的 serious ADE reports，或新／舊 drugs 過去未曾報告的 novel adverse events。\n   a. FAERS database 設計為 medications、biologics 的 postmarketing surveillance tool。其他 therapies，例如 medical devices，有各自 reporting system。\n   b. CDC 與 FDA 共同贊助 VAERS，聚焦辨識美國核准 vaccines 相關 adverse events 的 potential signals。\n3. FAERS、VAERS 為 postmarketing surveillance systems，其 limitations 包含：\n   a. Adverse event 缺乏確定性（可能未驗證）。\n   b. Underreporting。\n   c. 較不嚴重 adverse events 可能更少報告。\n   d. 提供的 detail 可能不足。", "en": "ii. According to Karch and Lasagna (JAMA 1975;234:1236-41), ADRs are “any response to a drug that is noxious and unintended, and that occurs at doses used in humans for prophylaxis, diagnosis, or therapy, excluding failure to accomplish the intended purpose.” iii. The primary difference between the WHO and Karch and Lasagna ADR definitions is that, according to the WHO definition, therapeutic failures are an unintended effect and an ADR, whereas according to the definition by Karch and Lasagna, they are not. Both definitions refer to doses normally used in humans, which exclude overdoses and medication errors that exceed normal doses. iv. ADRs caused by therapeutic failures with easily retrievable, detailed data that document the drug and the therapeutic failure can significantly affect patient care.\n\nb. Examples by definition\n\ni. WHO\n\n(a) Clopidogrel failure to prevent an ischemic stroke\n\n(b) Enoxaparin failure to prevent deep vein thrombosis\n\n(c) Famotidine failure to prevent intravenous ketorolac-induced gastropathy\n\n(d) Pantoprazole failure to prevent GI bleeding from a stress ulcer in a critically ill patient ii. Karch and Lasagna\n\n(a) Lorazepam when used to treat anxiety may cause sedation.\n\n(1) This is an unintended adverse reaction; conversely, lorazepam for sedation in a critically ill patient is an intended effect, not an ADR.\n\n(b) Diphenhydramine causing unintended sedation when used to treat allergic rhinitis\n\n(1) This is an ADR; conversely, diphenhydramine as a sedative hypnotic is an intended effect, not an ADR.\n\nC. FDA-Reportable ADEs\n\n1. For reporting an ADE to the FDA, the FDA defines ADEs as serious adverse events related to drugs or devices in which “the patient outcome is death, life threatening (real risk of dying), hospitalization (initial or prolonged), disability (significant, persistent, or permanent), congenital anomaly, or required intervention to prevent permanent impairment or damage.” FDA-reportable serious ADEs may include the following:\n\na. Acetaminophen-induced hepatotoxicity\n\nb. Apixaban-induced intracranial hemorrhage\n\nc. Gentamicin-induced irreversible ototoxicity\n\n2. The FDA is also interested in serious ADE reports for newly marketed drugs or novel adverse events that have not previously been reported for new or old drugs.\n\na. The FDA Adverse Reporting System (FAERS) database is designed as a postmarking surveillance tool for medications and biologics. Other therapies, such as medical devices, have their own reporting system.\n\nb. The CDC and FDA co-sponsor the Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System (VAERS), which is focused on identifying potential signals for adverse events associated with vaccines licensed for use within the United States.\n\n3. The FAERS and VAERS databases are used as postmarketing surveillance systems. Limitations of FAERS and VAERS include the following:\n\na. Lack of certainty of adverse event (i.e., may not be validated)\n\nb. Underreporting\n\nc. Less severe adverse events likely to be underreported\n\nd. Potential lack of detail provided"}, {"page": 14, "zh": "4. Health care professionals、consumers 向 FDA 報告 ADEs 為自願性，可透過 1993 年開始的 MedWatch program。與 FDA-regulated medication、biologic、tobacco product、dietary supplement、cosmetic、medical device 有關的以下項目皆可報告：\n   a. Serious ADE。\n   b. Serious ADR。\n   c. Product quality problem。\n   d. Product use error。\n   e. Therapeutic inequivalence。\n   f. Therapeutic failure。\n5. Clinicians 可直接向 drug manufacturer 報告 ADE。Pharmaceutical manufacturer 有法律責任，對所有收到的 ADEs 與 reporter follow-up，並向 FDA 報告。\n6. Health care professionals 或 manufacturers 直接提交的 ADE 與 medication error reports 會輸入 FAERS database。\n7. FDA 若偵測 safety concern，可能需 regulatory action 保護公眾，例如更新 product labeling、限制 drug、向 health care professionals／consumers 傳達 safety concerns，或將 drug 下市。\n\nD. 設計 ADE Reporting Program\n1. 完整 ADE program 應有 policy、procedure，包含 ADE detection、reporting、management、surveillance、education guidelines。Appendix 1 為 ADE reporting form。\n2. 因 pharmacists 的 drug-induced diseases expertise，以及預防／處置 ADEs 的角色，經常擔任 ADE committee chair 或 co-chair。\n3. ADE committee 應為 interdisciplinary，由以下組成：\n   a. Prescribers。\n   b. Pharmacists。\n   c. Nurses。\n   d. Risk management personnel。\n   e. Quality assurance、performance improvement personnel。\n   f. Other health care providers。\n4. 通常 ADE committee 是 P&T committee 的 subcommittee，向 P&T committee 報告。\n5. 依需審查的 reports 與 actionable items 數量，每月、每兩月或每季開會。\n6. ADE data 可由 specialty unit／service（例如 emergency medicine、ICU）報告。\n7. ADE data 可確保為該 specialty unit 建立適當 preventive measures。\n   a. 降低 ADE incidence 的 proactive method 為 FMEA，系統性主動辨識 system 中可能導致 ADE 的 potential failure points。\n   b. ADE 發生後，執行 root cause analysis；這是結構化確定 ADE 為何發生的 retroactive method，可協助辨識避免未來復發的方法。\n8. ADR、ADE data 應分為 mild、moderate、severe 報告。文獻有多種 scales，應建立各自 definitions。Box 1 提供範例。", "en": "4. Reporting of ADEs by health care professionals and consumers to the FDA is voluntary and may be done through the FDA’s MedWatch program (begun in 1993). All of the following may be reported with respect to an FDA-regulated medication, biologic, tobacco product, dietary supplement, cosmetic, or medical device:\n\na. Serious ADE\n\nb. Serious ADR\n\nc. Product quality problem\n\nd. Product use error\n\ne. Therapeutic inequivalence\n\nf. Therapeutic failure\n\n5. Clinicians may report an ADE directly to the drug’s manufacturer. The pharmaceutical manufacturer has a legal responsibility to follow up with the reporter on all ADEs reported and to report the ADE to the FDA.\n\n6. ADE reports and medication error reports submitted directly by health care professionals or manufacturers are entered in the FAERS database.\n\n7. If the FDA detects a safety concern, regulatory action may be needed to protect the public, such as updating the product labeling, restricting the drug, communicating the safety concerns to health care professionals and consumers, or removing the drug from the market.\n\nD. Designing an ADE Reporting Program\n\n1. A comprehensive ADE program should have a policy and procedure, with guidelines for ADE detection, reporting, management, surveillance, and education. Appendix 1 shows an ADE reporting form.\n\n2. Because of pharmacists’ expertise in drug-induced diseases and their role in preventing and managing ADEs, pharmacists often serve as chair or co-chair of the ADE committee.\n\n3. The ADE committee should be interdisciplinary and composed of the following:\n\na. Prescribers\n\nb. Pharmacists\n\nc. Nurses\n\nd. Risk management personnel\n\ne. Quality assurance and performance improvement personnel\n\nf. Other health care providers\n\n4. In general, the ADE committee is a subcommittee of the pharmacy and therapeutics (P&T) committee that reports to the P&T committee.\n\n5. The ADE committee should meet monthly, bimonthly, or quarterly, depending on the number of reports and actionable items that need review.\n\n6. ADE data can be reported by a specialty unit or service (e.g., emergency medicine, ICU).\n\n7. ADE data can ensure that appropriate preventive measures are developed for that specialty unit.\n\na. A proactive method to reduce the incidence of ADEs is a failure mode and effects analysis (FMEA), which is a proactive, structured method to identify potential failure points within a system that may lead to an ADE.\n\nb. If an ADE has occurred, performance of a root cause analysis, which is a structured method to determine why an ADE occurred (i.e., retroactive), can help identify methods to prevent the recurrence of the ADE in the future.\n\n8. ADR and ADE data should be reported as mild, moderate, or severe. Many scales are available in the literature. Definitions for each should be established. Box 1 provides an example."}, {"page": 15, "zh": "9. ADE committee 應：\n   a. 指定哪些 ADEs 可預防，說明為何可預防，並確定可能 preventive measures。\n   b. 確定哪些 ADEs 向 FDA 或 manufacturer 報告。\n   c. 報告誰偵測、誰報告、紀錄、處置 ADEs 的資料。\n   d. 依 drug／drug class 及 specialty units 提供 trending data。\n   e. 與醫院自身過去年份 ADEs benchmarking，並與 biomedical literature 發表的其他醫院資料比較。Total ADE data 可按以下報告：\n      i. ADEs 總數。\n      ii. 每次 admission 的 ADEs。\n      iii. 每位 patient 的 ADEs。\n      iv. 每 patient-days 的 ADEs。\n      v. 每 doses dispensed 的 ADEs。\n      vi. 每 doses administered 的 ADEs。\n10. 常見報告方法以 total admissions 為分母，可接受 benchmark 2.5%–10%（例如 10,000 admissions 的醫院報告 1000 ADEs，即 10% ADE reporting rate）。ADE benchmarks 難以確定，因多項 variables 影響 reporting methods，例如 facility 或 reporter 使用的 ADE definition、可報告的 clinical pharmacists 人數、reporting systems 的 vigilance 與 emphasis、以 technology 或 reports 增加 reporting。\n\nE. 在病人 Medical Record 紀錄 ADRs 與 ADEs\n1. Allergy data 一律紀錄於 medical record，patient profile 必須包含。最好紀錄引起 allergy 的特定 medication、reaction 類型、發生時間。\n2. 常存放這些資料的 EHRs，由「meaningful use」提供 incentives。CMS program 旨在協助採用 EHR technology，包括 advanced data sharing、聚焦 quality of care。2018 年 meaningful use 改稱 promoting interoperability。\n\n### Box 1. ADR／ADE Severity 程度定義ᵃ\n1. Mild ADR／ADE：提高 patient monitoring 需求，可伴隨或不伴隨 vital signs 變化，但最終無 patient harm；或任何導致增加 laboratory monitoring 的 adverse event。\n2. Moderate ADR／ADE：需要 antidotes 積極 intervention 或增加 hospital LOS（例如 severe hypotension〔BP <90／50 mm Hg〕、需 transfusions 的 bleeding）。\n3. Severe ADR／ADE：導致 patient harm、prolonged hospitalization、轉至較高 care level、permanent organ damage（例如 irreversible hepatotoxicity、renal failure），或 death，且 ADE causality nomogram score 為 probable。\nᵃ 另有其他 ADE severity scales。\nADE = adverse drug event；ADR = adverse drug reaction；BP = blood pressure。", "en": "9. The ADE committee should\n\na. Designate which ADEs are preventable, explain why they are preventable, and determine possible preventive measures\n\nb. Determine which ADEs will be reported to the FDA or the manufacturer\n\nc. Report the data regarding who is detecting ADEs and who reports, documents, and manages the ADEs\n\nd. Provide trending data on the basis of either drug or drug class and by specialty units\n\ne. Benchmark the hospital’s ADEs against itself in previous years and compare them with the data from other hospitals published in the biomedical literature. Total ADE data can be reported according to the following:\n\ni. Total number of ADEs ii. ADEs per admission iii. ADEs per patient iv. ADEs per patient-days\n\nv. ADEs per doses dispensed vi. ADEs per doses administered\n\n10. A popular method of reporting ADEs is by the total number of admissions, with an acceptable benchmark of 2.5%–10% (e.g., a hospital with 10,000 admissions reporting 1000 ADEs would have a 10% ADE reporting rate). ADE benchmarks are difficult to determine because of the many variables that affect the reporting methods, such as the ADE definition used by the facility or the definition used by the reporter, the number of clinical pharmacists available to report, the vigilance and emphasis of the reporting systems, and the use of technology or reports to increase reporting.\n\nE. Documenting ADRs and ADEs in the Patient’s Medical Record\n\n1. Allergy data are always documented in the patient’s medical record and are required in the patient profile. Preferably, the specific medication that caused the allergy, the type of reaction, and when the allergy occurred should be documented.\n\n2. The EHRs where these data are often found were incentivized by “meaningful use.” The Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services program is meant to assist in adopting EHR technology, including advanced data sharing and a focus on quality of care. In 2018, the term meaningful use was changed to promoting interoperability.\n\n1. Mild ADR or ADE: Results in heightened need for patient monitoring with or without a change in vital signs, but no ultimate patient harm, or any adverse event that results in the need for increased laboratory monitoring\n\n2. Moderate ADR or ADE: Results in the need for aggressive intervention with antidotes or increased length of hospital stay (e.g., severe hypotension [e.g., BP < 90/50 mm Hg], bleeding necessitating transfusions)\n\n3. Severe ADR or ADE: Results in harm to the patient, prolonged hospitalization, transfer to a higher level of care, permanent organ damage (e.g., irreversible hepatotoxicity or renal failure), or death with probable ADE causality nomogram score Box 1. Definitions for the Degree of ADR or ADE Severitya aOther ADE severity scales are available. ADE = adverse drug event; ADR = adverse drug reaction; BP = blood pressure."}]);
